{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>And the children of Israel journeyed from Beeroth Bene-Jaakan to Moserah.</b> This verse entered in this place because he said, \"and I prayed also on behalf of Aaron at that time,\" and he said, for Aaron did not die except in the fortieth year, and the proof is as his explanation says, that he said, \"at that time YHWH separated the tribe of Levi,\" and this was at Mount Sinai as I explained in the section of Wayyedabber. And our predecessors said they journeyed backward; and what is done with the word שם, \"there Aaron died and was buried there\"? And R. Isaac Ibn Ghayyat of blessed memory, the Sephardi, explained שם as meaning \"then,\" like it, \"from there the shepherd\" — and there is no need. Only, according to my opinion, Beeroth Bene-Jaakan is not Bene-Jaakan, and Moserah is not Moseroth; rather Kadesh is called Beeroth Bene-Jaakan, and there, the wilderness of Mount Hor is Moserah. And there are many such instances — do you not see that Scripture says \"wilderness of Shur\" and in another verse \"wilderness of Etham\"?"], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :notes {0 "Reading וקדמונינו for the printed וקדמונינו; the word נעש' resolved as נעשה. Ibn Ghayyat's proof phrase משם רוע׳ is truncated, apparently citing Genesis 46:34 משם רעיך; rendered hyper-literally as given."}, :source-witnesses [["On Your Way" "http://mobile.tora.ws/"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:23:02.047505220Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}